POSTS.append(dict(
 slug="is-hipages-worth-it",
 card="hipages plans, what a lead really costs once it is shared with other tradies, the sum to do before your contract renews, and what to build instead.",
 title="Is hipages Worth It? The Real Cost Per Job (2026)",
 desc="Is hipages worth it in 2026? What hipages plans and leads really cost per won job, the 12 month contract catch, and how Australian trades get work without it.",
 h1="Is hipages worth it? *The real cost per job.*",
 lede="hipages can be worth it for a new business in a big city that needs jobs on the calendar and can call every lead straight back. For most established trades it is an expensive way to win small jobs, because each lead is shared with other tradies and the monthly plan runs whether the phone rings or not. Here is how to check your own numbers.",
 ticks=["Current hipages plans and lead costs", "The cost per won job sum", "What to do before your contract renews"],
 html="""<h2 id="short-answer">Is hipages worth it for tradies?</h2><p>It depends on the size of your jobs and how fast you respond. hipages charges a monthly subscription for a set number of credits, and you spend those credits to contact homeowners who post a job. Several businesses can contact the same homeowner, so you are competing on speed and price from the first minute.</p><p>Tradies on forums describe it the same way again and again: useful to get off the ground, then dropped once referrals and Google reviews carry the business. The ones who stay tend to do larger jobs, where one win pays for months of credits.</p><h2 id="hipages-cost">How much does hipages cost in 2026?</h2><p>hipages does not publish a dollar price per lead. What is publicly tracked and widely reported looks like this:</p><ul><li><strong>Plans:</strong> tracked monthly plans run from about $249 to $649 a month, plus GST, with higher tiers for bigger businesses.</li><li><strong>Lead cost:</strong> independent estimates put a typical lead at roughly $25 to $80, depending on the trade, the job and the area.</li><li><strong>Contract:</strong> plans are commonly sold on a 12 month term, so the renewal date matters as much as the price.</li><li><strong>Sharing:</strong> each lead usually goes to more than one business, commonly reported as three to five.</li></ul><h2 id="cost-per-job">What a hipages job really costs you</h2><p>The useful number is not cost per lead, it is cost per won job:</p><p><strong>Cost per won job</strong> = (monthly plan + any extra credits) ÷ jobs you actually won that month.</p><p>A simple example: a $249 plan buys roughly seven leads in a category priced at about 42 credits each. Win one in five and you book about 1.4 jobs, so each job costs you around $178 in platform fees alone. That is fine on a $6,000 bathroom. On a $250 call out it wipes out the profit.</p><p>The same sum at different spends and win rates (examples):</p><table><thead><tr><th>Monthly spend</th><th>Leads</th><th>Win rate</th><th>Cost per job</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>$249</td><td>7</td><td>1 in 5</td><td>about $178</td></tr><tr><td>$449</td><td>12</td><td>1 in 5</td><td>about $187</td></tr><tr><td>$449</td><td>12</td><td>1 in 10</td><td>about $374</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Add the time spent driving out to quote jobs you never win, and the real figure is higher again.</p><h2 id="when-worth-it">When hipages is worth it</h2><ul><li>You are new, have no reviews yet and need work this month.</li><li>Your average job is large: renovations, installs, landscaping projects.</li><li>Someone can answer and call back within minutes, every day.</li><li>You use it to fill quiet weeks, not as your only source of work.</li></ul><h2 id="alternatives">hipages alternatives that you own</h2><p>The cheapest lead is the one that comes straight to you and nobody else. The same four things come up in every tradie forum:</p><ol><li><strong>Google Business Profile.</strong> Free, and it puts you on Google Maps where locals search. Use our <a href="/guides/google-business-profile-checklist/">Google Business Profile checklist</a>.</li><li><strong>Google reviews.</strong> Text a review link to every happy customer, including the ones who found you on hipages.</li><li><strong>Your own website.</strong> A fast site with a page for each service and suburb turns searches into calls only you receive. See <a href="/guides/what-a-trade-website-needs/">what a trade website needs</a>.</li><li><strong>Builders and repeat clients.</strong> Look after the builders, property managers and designers who can send steady work.</li></ol><h2 id="leaving">How to leave hipages without a gap in work</h2><ul><li>Find your contract end date and the cancellation terms before you do anything else.</li><li>Get your Google Business Profile and website working a few weeks before the plan ends.</li><li>Ask every customer you won through the platform for a Google review.</li><li>Ask every new caller how they found you, so you know what is working.</li></ul>""",
 checklist=["Pull three to six months of hipages invoices", "Count the jobs you actually won from them", "Divide your spend by those jobs", "Compare it with your profit on a typical job", "Find your contract end date", "Fill out your Google Business Profile", "Ask every happy customer for a Google review", "Get a website that sends calls only to you"],
 faq=[("How much does hipages cost per month?", "Publicly tracked plans run from about $249 to $649 a month plus GST, with credits included. Lead prices are not published and vary by trade and area."),
      ("How many tradies get the same hipages lead?", "Usually more than one. Tradies and independent reviews commonly report three to five businesses contacting the same homeowner."),
      ("Can I cancel hipages anytime?", "Plans are commonly sold on a 12 month term. Check your own agreement for the end date and cancellation terms before you sign or renew."),
      ("What is the best alternative to hipages?", "Leads you own: a complete Google Business Profile, steady Google reviews, a fast website with service and suburb pages, and repeat work from builders and past clients."),
      ("Is hipages worth it for electricians or plumbers?", "It can be for larger jobs like switchboard upgrades, solar or hot water installs. For small call outs the platform fee often costs more than the job makes.")],
 links=["is-angi-worth-it", "is-checkatrade-worth-it", "/guides/website-cost-australia/", "/guides/local-seo-for-trades/"],
))

POSTS.append(dict(
 slug="is-checkatrade-worth-it",
 card="Checkatrade, MyBuilder and Bark compared: what each really costs, how many trades see the same lead, and the sum that tells you if it pays.",
 title="Is Checkatrade Worth It? vs MyBuilder and Bark (2026)",
 desc="Is Checkatrade worth it in 2026? What Checkatrade, MyBuilder and Bark really cost UK tradespeople per won job, how leads are shared, and what to do instead.",
 h1="Is Checkatrade worth it? *Checkatrade, MyBuilder and Bark compared.*",
 lede="Checkatrade can pay for itself if you do bigger jobs in a busy area and the badge wins you trust. MyBuilder and Bark are cheaper to start but every lead is shared, so the real cost sits in the jobs you lose. The only way to know is to work out what one won job costs you on each.",
 ticks=["What each platform charges in 2026", "How many trades see each lead", "The cost per won job sum"],
 html="""<h2 id="short-answer">Is Checkatrade worth it?</h2><p>For some UK tradespeople, yes. The vetting badge carries weight with homeowners, and on large jobs like kitchens, bathrooms and extensions one win covers a lot of membership. For small jobs it rarely adds up, and the 12 month term means a bad decision costs you for a year.</p><p>MyBuilder and Bark work differently: no big monthly fee, but you pay each time you want to quote, and the same job is shown to several trades. Here is how they compare.</p><h2 id="costs">What Checkatrade, MyBuilder and Bark cost in 2026</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Platform</th><th>How you pay</th><th>Reported cost</th><th>Shared?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Checkatrade</td><td>Monthly membership, usually 12 months</td><td>From £30 + VAT (no leads at that tier); members report around £60 to £180 a month for plans with leads, depending on trade and area</td><td>Varies by plan</td></tr><tr><td>MyBuilder</td><td>Fee when a homeowner shortlists you</td><td>About £2 to £35 per shortlist, depending on job size</td><td>Several trades shortlisted</td></tr><tr><td>Bark</td><td>Credits per lead</td><td>£1.80 + VAT per credit; most leads roughly £7 to £40</td><td>Up to about 5 professionals</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Two details catch people out. Bark credits bought since November 2025 expire after three months. And on MyBuilder you pay for the chance to quote, not for the job, so a homeowner who goes quiet still costs you the fee.</p><h2 id="the-sum">The sum that tells you if it pays</h2><p><strong>Cost per won job</strong> = what you spent on the platform ÷ jobs you actually won from it.</p><p>Pay £20 a lead and win one in five, and each job cost £100. Win one in ten and it cost £200. On a £2,000 bathroom, £100 is fine. On a £90 call out, it is the whole job.</p><p>Do this for the last three to six months, platform by platform, and then compare each figure with your profit on a typical job. The platform that looks cheapest per lead is often not the cheapest per job.</p><h2 id="which">Checkatrade vs MyBuilder vs Bark: which is better?</h2><ul><li><strong>Checkatrade</strong> suits established trades doing larger jobs who value the badge and can commit for a year.</li><li><strong>MyBuilder</strong> suits trades who want to pick jobs one at a time with no standing fee, and who are happy to compete on quotes.</li><li><strong>Bark</strong> suits businesses that respond fast and quote cheaply on small jobs, but watch the credit expiry.</li></ul><p>Whichever you choose, start with the option you can stop tomorrow, not a 12 month term.</p><h2 id="own-your-leads">What to build instead</h2><ol><li><strong>Google Business Profile.</strong> Free, and it is where most local customers look first. Our <a href="/guides/google-business-profile-checklist/">Google Business Profile checklist</a> covers every setting.</li><li><strong>Google reviews you own.</strong> Reviews on a platform stay on the platform. Reviews on Google follow your business.</li><li><strong>Your own website.</strong> A page for each service and town, real photos and a tap to call button. See <a href="/guides/what-a-trade-website-needs/">what a trade website needs</a>.</li><li><strong>Referrals.</strong> Ask every happy customer, and stay in touch with past clients.</li></ol>""",
 checklist=["List every platform you pay for", "Add up three to six months of spend on each", "Count the jobs won from each one", "Work out cost per won job for each", "Check your Checkatrade renewal date", "Use up or stop buying Bark credits before they expire", "Set up and complete your Google Business Profile", "Ask every happy customer for a Google review"],
 faq=[("How much does Checkatrade cost per month?", "The entry plan starts from £30 + VAT a month but does not include leads. Members commonly report paying around £60 to £180 a month for plans with leads, depending on trade and area."),
      ("Is MyBuilder free for tradespeople?", "It is free to join. You pay a fee, typically about £2 to £35, when a homeowner shortlists you, whether or not you win the job."),
      ("How much does a Bark lead cost?", "Credits cost £1.80 + VAT and most leads need between about 5 and 20 credits, so roughly £7 to £40 a lead. Each lead can go to up to about five professionals."),
      ("Can I leave Checkatrade early?", "Memberships are usually on a 12 month term. Check your agreement for the end date and notice period before you sign or renew."),
      ("What is better than Checkatrade?", "For many trades, a complete Google Business Profile with steady reviews and a website of your own. They cost nothing per lead and the customers come to you alone.")],
 links=["is-angi-worth-it", "is-hipages-worth-it", "/guides/website-cost-uk/", "/guides/local-seo-for-trades/"],
))

POSTS.append(dict(
 slug="are-local-services-ads-worth-it",
 card="How Google Local Services Ads charge, what a lead costs in 2026, and the three free things to fix before you spend a dollar on ads.",
 title="Are Google Local Services Ads Worth It? (2026)",
 desc="Are Google Local Services Ads worth it for contractors? What LSA leads cost in 2026, how disputes work, LSA vs Google Ads, and the free fixes to make first.",
 h1="Are Local Services Ads worth it? *Fix three free things first.*",
 lede="For many contractors in the USA and Canada, Google Local Services Ads are one of the better paid options: you pay per lead, not per click, and the ad sits at the very top of the search. But they work far better when your free Google presence is already strong. Fix those first, then test a small budget and track booked jobs.",
 ticks=["What LSA leads cost in 2026", "LSA vs regular Google Ads", "The three free fixes that make ads cheaper"],
 html="""<h2 id="short-answer">Are Google Local Services Ads worth it?</h2><p>Often, yes, for contractors who answer the phone quickly and have good reviews. Local Services Ads charge when a customer calls, messages or books you through the ad, not when someone clicks. The lead also comes to you alone, which is a big difference from shared lead platforms.</p><p>They are a poor fit if your calls go to voicemail, your reviews are thin, or your profile is half finished, because Google ranks and prices ads partly on profile quality.</p><h2 id="cost">How much do Local Services Ads cost in 2026?</h2><p>Google does not publish a fixed price. You pay per valid lead and the price changes with your trade, your area and the time of year.</p><ul><li>A February 2026 industry benchmark across 888 contractors put the average home services lead at about <strong>$53</strong>, with general contractors much higher.</li><li>The same data showed roughly 44% of leads booking, which works out to about $233 per paying customer.</li><li>Bad leads (spam, wrong service, outside your area) can be disputed. Google's help pages say credit requests are not considered after 30 days, so check your leads weekly.</li></ul><p>Google is also moving Local Services Ads into Google Ads as a pay per lead campaign type during 2026. The pay per lead model stays, but the settings look different.</p><h2 id="lsa-vs-google-ads">Local Services Ads vs regular Google Ads</h2><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Local Services Ads</th><th>Google Search Ads</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>You pay for</td><td>A call, message or booking</td><td>A click</td></tr><tr><td>Where it shows</td><td>Top of local search</td><td>Search results and more</td></tr><tr><td>Needs a website?</td><td>No, but reviews and profile matter</td><td>Yes, a page that converts</td></tr><tr><td>Small budget</td><td>Workable</td><td>Hard; clicks add up fast</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Contractors in forums give the same advice about regular ads: a few hundred dollars spread across every service and town gets almost nothing. Narrow it to one service in a handful of towns, send people to a page about that service, and judge it on booked jobs, not clicks.</p><h2 id="fix-first">Three free things to fix before you spend $1</h2><ol><li><strong>Your Google Business Profile.</strong> Right category, every service, real photos, service areas and hours. Use our <a href="/guides/google-business-profile-checklist/">checklist</a>.</li><li><strong>Your reviews.</strong> A steady flow of recent reviews makes every ad and every free listing work harder. Text a review link the day a job finishes.</li><li><strong>Where people land.</strong> If an ad or a listing sends people to a slow, vague homepage, they leave. A fast page for each service with a tap to call button keeps them. See <a href="/guides/what-a-trade-website-needs/">what a trade website needs</a>.</li></ol><h2 id="how-to-test">How to test Local Services Ads properly</h2><ul><li>Start with one or two services you most want more of.</li><li>Answer every call live during working hours. Missed calls are paid for and wasted.</li><li>Dispute invalid leads every week.</li><li>After a month, divide what you spent by the jobs you booked. That is the only number that matters.</li></ul>""",
 checklist=["Complete every field of your Google Business Profile", "Get at least a few recent Google reviews", "Make sure each main service has its own web page", "Start Local Services Ads on one or two services only", "Answer every ad call live", "Dispute bad leads every week", "Track booked jobs, not leads or clicks", "Review cost per booked job after a month"],
 faq=[("Do you pay per click with Local Services Ads?", "No. You pay when a customer contacts you through the ad by calling, messaging or booking. Clicks that never contact you cost nothing."),
      ("How much is a Local Services Ads lead?", "Google does not publish prices. A 2026 benchmark across hundreds of contractors put the average home services lead at about $53, with big differences by trade and city."),
      ("Can I get money back for bad Local Services leads?", "You can dispute leads that are spam, outside your area or for work you do not do. Google says requests are not considered after 30 days."),
      ("Do I need a website for Local Services Ads?", "Not to run them, but customers often check your website and reviews before they call. A strong site and profile help you win the lead you paid for."),
      ("Is $500 a month enough for Google Ads?", "For regular search ads it is tight. Focus it on one service in a few towns, or start with Local Services Ads where you pay per lead instead of per click.")],
 links=["is-angi-worth-it", "how-to-get-more-customers", "/guides/local-seo-for-trades/", "/guides/google-business-profile-checklist/"],
))
